ut people  we know only casually with ties tend to  have much different networks of friends  and other acquaintances it's been  well-documented that for this reason  weak ties are incredibly valuable when  it comes to  looking for a job but even more  important than having a lot of weak  tides is happened the right weak ties  here's a real world example this  principle in action when I first wanted  to write a book I didn't know a lot of  other authors I could lean on for advice  I dutifully searched online and went to  the library to research books in the  process of connecting with an agent or  publisher I then set out the contact  editors and agents myself what I didn't  get very far most people simply didn't  reply that my crews when that approach  didn't work I started asking everyone I  knew if they had any advice or  connections they could pass on I finally  connected with a guy who would soon  become my literary agent David - a  referral from one of his other authors  who's been reading my blog unbeknownst  to me seven years later David and I have  worked together on full of books I've  also been able to refer several other  successful authors to him in a way  meeting David was a human equivalent of  a winning casino game it's a very strong  relationship that was initially for him  a weak tie the example is relevant for  much more than writing books success  isn't found completely in persistence  it's found in working hard and smart try  try again  sure but try again in a strategic manner  the point is at justice with applying to  jobs when it comes to building  relationships  quality matters over quantity the goal  in other words is not necessarily to  connect with more people but to connect  with more of the right kinds of people  stop playing the numbers game  you
 

